The term "Correctional Officers" shall refer to:

(a) Law Enforcement Officers of the Lee County Sheriff's Office.

(b) Civilian employees designated as Correctional Deputies by the Lee Cοunty Sheriff's Office.

The term “Convict” and “Inmate” shall interchangably refer to:

(a) Individuals convicted of a crime and sentenced to a term of imprisonment, or arrested for a set period of time by a sworn peace officer.

The term “Warden” shall refer to:

(a) The employee designated by the County Sheriff to manage all Correctional Deputies.

The term “Contraband” within the meaning of 1 M.S.C. § 1709 shall refer to:

(a) Any item not authorized by facility/warden rules, regulations, or law, including but not limited to weapons, controlled substances, electronic communication devices, escape tools, or any item introduced with the intent to aid in escape, concealment, or disruption of facility operations.

The term “Introduces” within the meaning of 1 M.S.C. § 1709 shall refer to:

(a) Any attempt to bring contraband into a facility through physical delivery, mail, drop-off, or other means.

The term “Manufactures” within the meaning of 1 M.S.C. § 1709 shall refer to:

(a) The alteration, construction, or creation of contraband from permitted items within the facility.

There shall be in a complex known as the Lee County Jail, which shall be located at Rikers Island in Lee County, Mayflower, and which shall consist of the property under the jurisdiction of the Lee County Sheriffs Office.

Such institution shall be a correctional facility for males and females set forth in this subchapter.

MCC Lee shall be classified as a mixed security correctional facility, to be used for the following functions:

Probation Officers may conduct warrantless searches of any individual actively on probation or parole, or on pretrial release as specified by court order. These searches must be equally applied to individuals on probation, and may not be targeted unless with probable cause.

Individuals on probation, parole, or on pretiral release as specified by court order:

(a) If arrested, notify the arresting law enforcement agency of their status as a person on probation/parole/pretrial release.

(b) Refrain from posessing or carrying any weapons, including firearms.

(c) Refrain from the commission of further criminal offenses of any level.

(d) Adhere to lawful directions given by Probation Officers, including to meet with the Probation Officer at specified locations and times.

(e) Comply with any searches done by Probation Officers or law enforcement officers.

(f) Refrain from removing any electronic monitoring devices placed on ones person.

(g) Refrain from associating with any individual with 3+ felony records, or who is actively on probation.

No individual shall be permitted entry into the Lee County Jail unless they have been authorized by a Correctional Officer and one of the following applies:

(a) They are visiting an inmate, and the inmate has consented to the visit;

(b) They are a legal representative of an inmate;

(c) They are a law enforcement officer who has either been requested by Correctional Officers or is responding to an emergency situation;

(d) They are an emergency medical services provider, firefighter, or other emergency responder who was requested to respond by Correctional Officers; or

(e) They are otherwise authorized by a Correctional Officer.

The Warden may create rules and regulations for individuals who enter the Lee County Jail, which must be publicly accessible. Correctional Officers shall have the sole authority to enforce these rules.

Correctional Officers shall have the authority to remove any individual from the County Jail should the individual:

(a) Fail to comply with a lawful order given by Correctional Officers,

(b) Infringe on the visiting rules of the Lee County Jail or violate any other rule or regulation promulgated by the Warden; or

(c) Act in a manner which threatens the security or safety of the Lee County Jail or its inmates; including committing or being suspected of committing a criminal offense within the grounds of the Lee County Jail.

Civilian Correctional Officers may, when on duty and in accordance with departmental guidelines:

(a) Detain any individual for up to twelve (12) minutes, or until the arrival of a sworn law enforcement officer, whichever occurs first, if the individual fails to comply with a lawful order to leave the Lee County Jail; commits or is suspected of committing a criminal offense (violations of Title 1 of the M.S.C.) within the grounds of the Lee County Jail or immediately adjacent thereto; commits a criminal offense against, within the vicinity of, or in connection with a prisoner work operation or community service operation supervised by Correctional Officers; commits or is suspected of committing a violation of 1 M.S.C. § 1311 Escape anywhere within the State; or if instructed to do so by a sworn law enforcement officer. In addition to the authority described above, a Civilian Correctional Officer may only detain an individual outside of the county jail grounds or prisoner work operations when it is immediately necessary to prevent death or serious bodily injury, no other reasonable alternatives exist, and sworn law enforcement officers are not immediately available. Civilian Correctional Officers must immediately notify and transfer custody to a sworn law enforcement officer as soon as practicable after detaining any individual under this subsection;

(b) Carry on their person and utilize, in accordance with provisions of State law the following articles of equipment and their respective ammunition or otherwise essential components; extendable, fixed and straight batons, stun guns or taser devices, pepper-spray or other similar chemical self-defense items, encrypted handheld radios, fixed or chain connected handcuffs; and department issued firearms;

(c) If authorized to do so by a sworn law enforcement officer close or block public roadways, establish, maintain, and assist in the operation of checkpoints and otherwise aid in the public service of the State;

(d) Use a reasonable amount of force to overcome resistance, prevent evasion, and facilitate their lawful duties;

(i) Civilian Correctional Officers shall not initiate or engage in vehicular pursuits unless they are passengers in a vehicle operated by a sworn law enforcement officer.

(e) Search any person, aircraft, or vehicle which seeks access to or is within the Lee County Jail.

(f) Perform any other reasonable duties or functions at the discretion of the Warden.

Correctional Officers are authorized to use deadly force against a prisoner when the officer reasonably believes such force is necessary to prevent the commission of a violation of 1 M.S.C. § 1311 Escape. This authority is given when the prisoner is confined within a prison, jail, road camp, work operation, or other penal institution, or is being transported to or from such a facility or place, and the officer reasonably believes the prisoner is escaping or attempting to escape from lawful confinement. Deadly force may only be used if the officer has made a reasonable effort, when feasible, to warn the prisoner that deadly force will be used unless the escape attempt ceases immediately and only if the officer reasonably believes that no other means are available to prevent the escape.

The term “Corrections Officer” shall refer to:

(a) A county or state probation agent or other corrections employee.

