(a) the Chancellor, who shall be an ex officio member;

(b) one member appointed by the Chancellor among the alumni;

(c) two members, none of whom shall be students, members of the administrative staff or members of the teaching staff, appointed by the Governor; and

(d) three members elected by the administrative staff among the administrative staff.

Members of the Governing Council shall act with diligence, honesty, and with good faith in the best interests of the University.

No person shall serve as a member of the Governing Council unless they are a resident of the State.

The Governor, with the advice and consent of the Senate, shall appoint the Chancellor.

The Chancellor and each member of the Governing council shall serve for one hundred and eighty days, with each term commencing upon the commencement of the Chancellor’s term.

Where a vacancy occurs for any reason among the Governing Council, the Chancellor shall appoint a person to fill the vacancy for the remainder of the Governing Council’s term.

Four members shall constitute a quorum of the Governing Council.

(a) In times where the Governing Council does not have enough members to constitute a quorum, the Chancellor shall assume all rights and responsibilities normally assigned to the Governing Council for the duration of the Governing Council’s term.

The government, management and control of the University, and of the property, revenues, business and affairs thereof, are vested in the Governing Council, and, without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the Governing Council has power to:

(a) appoint, promote, suspend and remove members of the teaching and administrative staffs of the University, as the Governing Council considers necessary, but no member of the teaching staff shall be appointed, promoted, suspended or removed unless recommended by the Chancellor or such other employee of the University designated therefor by the Chancellor under this section;

(b) fix the number, the duties and the salaries of the University;

(c) delegate such of its powers under clauses i and ii to the Chancellor as it considers necessary;

(d) provide for the granting of and grant degrees, including honorary degrees, diplomas and certificates;

(i) the Chancellor shall be provided the privilege of granting or revoking degrees to any resident of the State;

(e) borrow from time to time real property for the use of the University, and give such security against the assets of the University by way of mortgage or otherwise, as it determines;

(f) determine and regulate the standards for the admission of students to the University, the contents and curricula of all programs and courses of study and the requirements for graduation;

(g) delegate such of its powers under clauses iv, v and vi as it considers proper to the Chancellor; and

(h) do all such acts and things as are necessary or expedient for the conduct of its affairs and the affairs of the University.

The duties of a peace officer shall include the following but is not limited to:

(a) preserving the peace;

(b) preventing crimes and other offenses;

(c) assisting victims of crime;

(d) apprehending criminals and other offenders who may lawfully be taken into custody;

(e) participating in prosecutions;

(f) executing warrants that are to be executed by peace officers and performing related duties;

(g) performing lawful duties as promulgated by their respective department head or the Attorney General;

(h) completing training required by this act, the peace officer’s respective department, or a law enforcement training institute; and

(i) performing such other duties as assigned to the peace officer by this act or any other Act, including any prescribed duties.

A peace officer shall not, in the course of their duties, treat any person in a manner that contravenes the Constitution of the State of Mayflower.

A peace officer shall not, by act or omission, do anything that the peace officer, at the time, knows or reasonably ought to know would infringe or deny a person’s rights or freedoms listed in the Constitution of the State of Mayflower.

A peace officer shall not make an arrest if, at the time of the arrest, the peace officer knows or reasonably ought to know that the arrest is unlawful.

A peace officer shall not authorize or make a physical or psychological detention if, at the time of the detention, the peace officer knows or reasonably ought to know that the detention is unlawful.

A peace officer shall not neglect the health or safety of any person who is in their custody as a result of the peace officer’s duties.

A peace officer shall not use force unless:

(a) the force is used for the purpose of carrying out a lawful duty;

(b) the peace officer is entitled by statute or common law to use force for the purpose of carrying out that duty;

(c) the peace officer is acting on reasonable grounds; and

(d) the force used is no more than is necessary given the circumstances.

A peace officer shall not solicit, offer or take a bribe.

A peace officer shall not use their position as a peace officer to do any of the following:

(a) Use any information obtained through their position as a peace officer for personal advantage.

(b) Willingly and knowingly interfere with the administration of justice.

(c) Use departmental property or resources for personal or financial benefit.

No person shall be hired, carry out the duties, or act in the capacity of a peace officer unless he or she:

(b) is physically and mentally able to perform the duties of the position;

(c) does not have any pending criminal matter before any court of the State or the Union of Columbia in which the person is named as the defendant and has been reasonably notified of such matter;

(d) has not been convicted of any degree of misconduct within the last fifty (50) days;

(e) holds an active certification by a recognized law enforcement training institute; and

(f) is actively employed by a recognized law enforcement entity within the State for the purposes of keeping the peace.

If a person is convicted of any degree of misconduct, the sentencing judge may grant an exemption from compliance with 4 M.S.C. 5 § 1601(d), either in full or for a period of up to fifty (50) days.

The department head of a law enforcement agency shall have the authority to:

(a) establish or amend written procedures regarding the administration of their respective department;

(b) approve re-education training or disciplinary measures suggested by their respective department’s internal investigation unit; and

(c) delegate in writing any powers and duties in this act, or its regulations, to a member or office of their respective department, subject to any limitations, conditions or requirements set out in the delegation.

The department head of a law enforcement agency is responsible for:

(a) ensuring department policies and procedures are in compliance with all applicable laws and regulations;

(b) overseeing and managing all activities of their respective department;

(c) in writing, authorizing police operations in their respective department; and

(d) approving all offers of employment of their respective department.

When it is reasonably safe for a peace officer, every peace officer upon request from any person is required to:

(a) show their identification card;

(b) state the department they are acting in the capacity of; and

(c) if applicable, state their callsign.

A peace officer is not required to identify themselves if:

(a) it is not reasonably safe for the peace officer;

(b) the peace officer is operating an emergency service vehicle;

(c) the peace officer has already identified themselves to the same person within the last sixty (60) minutes;

(d) the peace officer has declared themselves out of service; or

(e) the peace officer is engaged in or participating in an authorized police operation where the act of providing identification would compromise the objective(s) of the operation.

Any individual in possession of a firearm with a modification made to, affixed to, or installed on that serves any of the following purposes shall be guilty of 1 M.S.C. § 6102: Unlicensed Possession of a Firearm;

(a) Barrel attachments or other modifications which suppress or otherwise silence the audible noise generated by the process of discharging a projectile.

(b) Modification to reduce the length of a firearms barrel to be less than three (3) studs in length, or to reduce the length of the firearm entirely to be less than six (6) studs.

All Government run departments and agencies under the executive branch(es) of government, including law enforcement agencies, within the state shall be obligated to create and publish a public database, that is easily accessible within a separate public documents or similarly titled channel within a department’s communication server, and has:

(a) a registry of employees and each employees:

(i) assigned unit/division;

(ii) current position;

(iii) rank;

(iv) callsign/badge number; and

(v) complete disciplinary history;

(b) a code of conduct with all punitive titles as defined by 4 M.S.C. 7 § 1003;

(c) a list of all discharges; and

(d) a list of all administrative actions.

Any department or agency head who fails to comply with this subchapter shall be liable for Gross Misconduct and shall be prosecuted by the Department of Justice.

Whilst the Controlled Burns & Explosives Office shall be entrusted with releasing safety regulations through their Burn Guide, the following details must be met for a vehicle to be eligible to be burnt;

(a) Vehicle contains 10 (ten) or more items defined by State Laws to be illegal for possession by the registered vehicle owner.

A vehicle with less than 10 (ten) items illegal for possession by the registered vehicle owner may be burnt by law enforcement officers only if the vehicle is a special vehicle registered to be owned by a member of a criminal street gang or terrorist organization, as declared by the Attorney General.

Any item within this section shall be known as Prohibited Goods.

It shall be illegal for an individual to be in unlawful possession of any goods listed in this subchapter.

The following items shall collectively be known as ‘Prohibited Goods, Class I’, or any other similar name

(a) Items, kits, or modifications tools that can be used to modify an automatic firearm;

(b) Items, kits, or modification tools which can cause a firearm that is not an automatic firearm to become so;

(c) Law enforcement exclusive equipment.

The following items shall collectively be known as ‘Prohibited Goods, Class II', or any other similar name:

(a) Items, kits, or modifications tools that can be used to modify a firearm;

(b) Crates containing illegal firearms;

(c) Crates containing illegal ammunition.

(d) Gavels; and

(e) Items which show membership, admittance or allegiance to a criminal organization or enterprise.

The following items shall collectively be known as ‘Prohibited Goods, Class III’, or any other similar name:

(a) Gold Bars;

(b) Silver Bars;

(c) Dirty Money;

(d) Encrypted Phone;

(e) Military Encryption Cards; and

(f) File of Military Intelligence.

(g) Underground Firearms Access Card.

(h) Lockpicks.

“Underground Firearms Access Card” shall refer to any physical or digital card, token, pass, code, or other medium designed, issued, or possessed for the primary purpose of granting or facilitating entry to any building, room, or premises where firearms, firearm parts, or firearm modification kits are offered, transferred, or sold in violation of the laws of the State of Mayflower. Possession of such a card shall be unlawful unless by a law-enforcement officer or other person acting under the authority of a law-enforcement agency in the course of official duties.

“Lockpick” shall refer to any device, tool, or instrument designed, adapted, or primarily used for the purpose of unlocking a lock without the use of the key or code intended by the lock’s manufacturer, except when possessed by a duly licensed locksmith, a person acting under the direction of a law-enforcement agency, or a person possessing such tools solely for lawful occupational, educational, or hobbyist purposes unrelated to any unlawful entry.

Insurance Businesses must clearly outline all types of Insurance Policies and its individual rules provided by the Insurance Business in a clear and discernable method, which is publicly accessible, which includes:

(a) How the Insurance Policy applies to the Policyholder;

(b) The Premium of the Insurance Policy;

(c) The term of coverage that the Policyholder receives for the Insurance Policy; and

(d) An example on how the Insurance Policy will compensate the Policyholder.

Every month, if a Policyholder submits a Claim to the Insurance Business, the Insurance Business must publish a summary claims report which is publicly accessible that includes:

(a) The total amount of Claims submitted by Policyholders;

(b) The total amount of Claims accepted by the Insurance Business;

(c) The total amount of Claims rejected by the Insurance Business, with their reason(s) for rejection;

(d) The total amount of Policyholders, including the difference from the previous public report;

(e) Any changes to an Insurance Policy;

(f) Any loans that the Insurance Business has obtained to finance the Insurance Business, and the amount of money the loan is worth;

(g) Any court convictions against the Insurance Business;

(h) Any commercial punishments against the Insurance Business by the Commerce Office; and

(i) A link to the prospective business report.

Every six months an Insurance Business must submit a summary claims report, regardless if a Claim was submitted by a Policyholder that month.

An Insurance Business may not:

(a) Use methods of threats, coercion, harassment or intimidation to collect a premium; and

(b) Offer an Insurance Policy where the Policyholder has no reasonable method to repay it.

An Insurance Business may only reject to compensate for a Claim submitted by a Policyholder where:

(a) The Policyholder misrepresents the facts in a Claim;

(b) The Policyholder fails to pay Premiums;

(c) The Policyholder requests the Claim as a result of their involvement in criminal activity;

(d) The Policyholder attempts to submit a Claim for something that is not covered by the Insurance Policy;

Such a denial must be outlined in writing, with reference to the relevant statute.

Transportation Service Companies shall be required to maintain a publicly accessible database of their drivers, including but not limited to the following:

(a) the driver's Roblox username;

(b) the driver's date of hire;

(c) the driver's position;

(d) the driver's registered vehicle model; and

(e) the license plate of the driver's registered vehicle model.

Transportation Service Companies shall only hire drivers that possess a Mayflower Driving License.

Transportation Service Companies shall be required to temporarily suspend the employment of drivers that have had their Mayflower Driving License revoked, until the repossession of the license by the driver.

Transportation Service Companies shall be obligated to implement policy that details potential administrative action against employees who in any instance have met all of either sections of the following criteria:

(a) the employee had a passenger in the vehicle;

(b) the employee drove recklessly and in a manner that endangered other drivers and pedestrians; or

(c) the employee was arrested for a Misdemeanour or Felony against the Mayflower Vehicle Code, whilst in the commission of his transportation service vehicle.

The liability of Transportation Service Companies over their employees shall depend on the type of business (as detailed and regulated by the Department of State) however TSCs that are not already liable for their employees shall be required to provide legal counsel and/or sufficient legal advice to drivers facing civil suits for actions committed whilst in the operation of their transportation service vehicle [provided that they are on-duty and representing the TSC employed by].

Arbitrary punishment is defined as:

(a) punishment that has no backing in regulation, policy, or law;

(b) punishment whose policy, law, or regulation prescribing it is ambiguous, or unclear, or a violation of a constitutional right or privilege or statutory enactment;

(c) punishment with no factual backing;

(d) punishment outside and beyond what is explicitly prescribed in policy; or

(e) punishment that takes no accounting of mitigating factors, circumstances, or liabilities.

Arbitrary punishment is prohibited.

The principles of the merit system shall be as follows:

(a) Recruitment should be from qualified individuals from appropriate sources in an endeavor to achieve a work force from all segments of society, and selection and advancement should be determined solely on the basis of relative ability, knowledge and skills, after fair and open competition which assures that all receive equal opportunity.

(b) All employees and applicants for employment should receive fair and equitable treatment in all aspects of personnel management without regard to political affiliation, race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, or handicapping condition, and with proper regard for their privacy and constitutional rights.

(c) All employees should maintain high standards of integrity, conduct, and concern for the public interest.

(d) The State workforce should be used efficiently and effectively.

(e) Employees should be retained on the basis of adequacy of their performance, inadequate performance should be corrected, and employees should be separated who cannot or will not improve their performance to meet required standards.

(f) Employees should be provided effective education and training in cases in which such education and training would result in better organizational and individual performance.

(g) Employees should be—

(i) protected against arbitrary action, personal favoritism, or coercion for political purposes; and

(ii) prohibited from using their official authority or influence for the purpose of interfering with or affecting the result of an election or a nomination for election.

Employees should be protected against reprisal for the lawful disclosure of information which the employees reasonably believe evidences-- a violation of any law, rule, or regulation, or mismanagement, a gross waste of resources, an abuse of authority, or a substantial and specific danger to public safety.

The attributes of the merit system shall be as follows:

(a) When making decisions of employment or promotion, officials shall review the following attributes of merit:

(i) logged patrol/work hours for the current cycle;

(ii) past performance in other departments;

(iii) abilities to work as a team and play well with others, which is affirmed by testimony of another leader or employee;

(iv) demonstration of sound judgment, which is affirmed by testimony of another leader;

(v) demonstration of leadership qualities while holding a position in the State of Mayflower, which is affirmed by testimony of another leader or employee;

(vi) effort in the completion of an application, cover letter, or letter of interest;

(vii) scores on an accredited exam, if applicable;

(viii) commendations or written letters of recommendation; and

(ix) holding a valid accreditation, which may be required for the position.

(x) Completion of formal state training or continued education relevant to the position.

Performance in another community or group shall not be used as a means to demonstrate the merit of an individual.

When making a hiring decision officials can use the following attributes to discredit and demerit a hire:

(a) bad performance in another department or agency;

(b) poor completion of an application, cover letter, or letter of interest;

(c) poor accredited exam scores;

(d) past disciplinary actions;

(e) character defects which would bring harm to the health of the department or community;

(f) failure to use discretion;

(g) inability to gain a security clearance, other than not requiring it in the first place;

(h) refusal to comply with regulations or lawful orders;

(i) terrible activity in a previous department or agency; or

(j) no, or little experience within the State of Mayflower.

During the hiring process, an agency shall indicate all attributes of merit and demerit and subjectively, according to their own principles, weigh each application.

A competitive hiring round shall be any hiring period where the number of spots to be taken in is clearly defined, publicly posted, and limited.

(a) A department may not intake more than the number of applicants they have posted in a competitive hiring round.

A non-competitive hiring round shall be any hiring round where the number of spots is not clearly defined, publicly posted, and not limited.

(a) If a person passes all preceding stages in a non-competitive hiring round, then they must be accepted as their outcome so long as this would not cause the department to exceed their allocated budget.

An application / character screening may only be denied for an attribute of demerit, and accepted for an attribute of merit. If they have neither then they shall be denied.

No individual may skip more than three (3) consecutive ranks from their current rank.

(a) If any Department is found promoting someone over three (3) ranks, the Department of Justice shall investigate such Department for any findings of Nepotism and any level of Misconduct.

(b) The Mayflower National Guard shall be exempt from paragraph 2510 of this subchapter.

sections.text:
No employee shall be subject to any adverse administrative action, be coerced, or in any way penalized for a failure to maintain activity or perform duties during a state holiday.

For the purposes of this section, a “state holiday” shall refer to:

(a) The week of Christmas, which is on the 25th Day of December;

(b) The week of New Year’s Eve, which is on the 31st of December;

(c) The week of American Thanksgiving, which is on the fourth Thursday of November;

(d) The week of Easter, which is on the first Sunday after the full moon, which occurs on or after the spring equinox.

(i) If the full moon falls on a Sunday, then the Easter holiday shall be on the next Sunday.

(e) The Sixth (6th) day of August shall be known as Tayvion Cole Rememberance Day, in honor of the great Bloxveldo-Columbian football player and rapper who was taken from this world far too soon.

(f) The fifth day of July (07/05) shall be known as Happy Day, as a way to improve the morale and happiness of all Mayflower citizens.

parts.text:
The State Bar has the authority to adopt, implement, and enforce rules governing the practice of law and the conduct of its members.

The State Bar shall establish and maintain the Mayflower Rules of Professional Conduct, which outline the ethical and professional responsibilities of all attorneys licensed in this State.

In consultation of its committee members, the State Bar may also adopt additional rules, regulations and procedures necessary for:

(a) Disciplining, suspending, or disbarring attorneys who violate professional standards or commit acts of misconduct;

(b) Administering and enforcing legal education requirements to maintain attorney competency and ethical practice;

(c) Resolving disputes between attorneys and their clients, including fee arbitration.

(d) All rules adopted by the State Bar are binding on members upon adoption and must be made publicly available. The Bar may amend or repeal rules as necessary.

Before adopting, amending, or repealing any rule, the State Bar shall:

(a) Provide notice of the proposed rule to all members and the public, specifying changes and inviting written comments;

(b) Consult with members if deemed necessary;

(c) Submit the proposed rule to the Supreme Court for final approval before it takes effect.

(d) All rules become effective once enacted and must be published in a way that is accessible to all members and the public.

The State Bar, acting as a precepting authority under the direction of the Director, may impose and enforce a monthly levy payable by all attorneys registered to practice law in the State of Mayflower.

The amount of the levy and the payment schedule shall be determined by the State Bar, subject to the approval of the Supreme Court of the State of Mayflower.

A levy cannot be imposed or amended without the support of at least 2/3rds of the sitting Justices of the Supreme Court, and it may only be imposed on the “Active” class of membership.

The levy must be used strictly to fund the administration and operation of the State Bar, including:

(a) regulatory activities;

(b) member services;

(c) educational programs;

(d) public protection initiatives;

(e) pro bono programs to assist indigent populations;

(f) reappropriation of funds into the Administrative Office of the State of Mayflower Courts;

(g) failure to pay the levy as prescribed may result in penalties, including suspension of membership; and

(h) in cases of financial hardship, the Bar may choose to waive fees for members.

The Supervising Justice may, at their discretion, initiate the removal or dismissal of the Chief Public Defender if it is determined that such action is necessary for the effective operation of the Office of Public Defenders.

Grounds for removal may include, but are not limited to:

(a) failure to perform duties effectively or competently;

(b) misconduct or breach of ethical obligations;

(c) inability to maintain the confidence and trust necessary for the proper functioning of the Office;

(d) any other reason that, in the judgment of the Supervising Justice, warrants removal in the best interest of the Office and its clients.

Before the Chief is removed or dismissed, the Supervising Justice must seek and obtain permission from the Chief Justice of the Supreme Court of Mayflower. Upon approval by the Chief Justice, the Supervising Justice shall have the authority to immediately remove the Chief from their position and appoint an interim Chief until a permanent replacement is selected. The Supervising Justice shall then initiate the process to appoint a new Chief Public Defender as provided under Sections of this act.

The Supreme Court may appoint a marshal, who shall be subject to removal by the Court, and may fix his compensation.

The marshal may, with the approval of the Chief Justice of the State of Mayflower, appoint and fix the compensation of necessary assistants and other employees to attend the Court, and necessary custodial employees under his supervison.

There shall be a Supreme Court Police Division established within a department, as designated by the Chief Justice of the State of Mayflower, with the approval of the Supreme Court of Mayflower and the Head of the relevant department that will house this division.

The marshal shall:

(a) attend the Court at its sessions;

(b) serve and execute all processes and orders issued by the Court or a member thereof;

(c) take charge of all property of the State of Mayflower used by the Court or its members;

(d) oversee the Supreme Court Police; and

(e) perform any act and take any measure necessary and proper for the efficient performance of the duties with the approval of the Supreme Court.

A defendant in any criminal action has the right of appeal under the rules hereinafter prescribed, and provided, however, before the defendant who has been convicted upon either his plea of guilty or plea of nolo contendere before the court and the court, upon the election of the defendant, assesses punishment and the punishment does not exceed the punishment recommended by the prosecutor and agreed to by the defendant and his attorney may prosecute his appeal, he must have permission of the trial court, except on those matters which have been raised by written motion filed prior to trial.

The State is entitled to appeal an order of a court in a criminal case if the order:

(a) dismisses an indictment, information, or complaint or any portion of an indictment, information, or complaint;

(b) arrests or modifies a judgment;

(c) grants a new trial;

(d) sustains a claim of former jeopardy; or

(e) grants a motion to suppress evidence, a confession, or an admission, if jeopardy has not attached in the case and if the prosecuting attorney certifies to the trial court that the appeal is not taken for the purpose of delay and that the evidence, confession, or admission is of substantial importance in the case.

The State is entitled to a stay in the proceedings pending the disposition of an appeal under 5 M.S.C. 1 § 1401.2.

Each magistrate appointed under this section shall have authority within the district where the appointing court holds sessions, at any other locations where that court may operate, and in any other areas as permitted by law, to:

(a) issue search warrants and arrest warrants upon a competent finding of probable cause;

(b) conduct hearings and trials, both for civil cases and criminal;

(c) hear evidence;

(d) compel production of relevant evidence, including books, papers, vouchers, documents, and other writings;

(e) rule on admissibility of evidence;

(f) issue summons and attachments for the appearance of witnesses;

(g) examine witnesses;

(h) swear witnesses for hearings and trials;

(i) perform any act and take any measure necessary and proper for the efficient performance of the duties assigned by the district court judge; and

(j) any or other power conferred or imposed upon Magistrates by any other law.

(a) motions for injunctive relief;

(b) motion for judgment on the pleadings;

(c) motions for summary judgment;

(d) motion to dismiss or quash an indictment or information made by the defendant;

(e) motion to suppress evidence;

(f) motion to dismiss;

(g) maintain a class action;

(h) motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim upon which relief may be granted; and

(i) motions for involuntary dismissal of an action.

A judge may reconsider any pretrial matter determined by a magistrate under this section where it is demonstrated that the magistrate’s decision is clearly erroneous or contrary to law.

A judge may, by order, designate a magistrate to conduct hearings, including evidentiary hearings, and to submit to the judge proposed findings of fact and recommendations for the disposition of any matter excepted under this section.

At the conclusion of any hearing conducted under this section, the magistrate shall transmit to the judge all papers and documents relating to the case, including the magistrate's findings of fact and recommendations;
a statement confirming that notice of the findings and recommendations, along with the right to request a hearing before the judge, has been duly given to all parties.

The magistrate shall file with the court any proposed findings and recommendations made under this section, and shall cause a copy thereof to be served upon all parties forthwith.

The judge shall adopt, modify, or reject the magistrate's recommendations no later than the fifth working day after the date the judge receives the recommendations. If the judge does not take action within the time provided by this subsection, the recommendations of the magistrate shall be deemed adopted by the judge.

Any party may, within fourteen (14) days of being served with a copy of the proposed findings and recommendations, file written objections thereto in accordance with the rules of court.

Upon consideration of any objections duly filed under this section, a judge shall make a de novo determination of the matters objected to, and may accept, reject, or modify, in whole or in part, the findings or recommendations made by the magistrate, receive further evidence, or remit the matter to the magistrate with directions.

A magistrate shall not be permitted or assigned to any category of cases that include:

(a) questions of vast economic and political significance;

(b) cases in which the state is seeking imposition of capital punishment; or

(c) cases that involve a significant and complex constitutional issue.

Cases in the Supreme Court may be reviewed by the Supreme Court through the following means, but not limited to:

(a) By writ of certiorari, which may be granted upon the petition of any party involved in a civil or criminal case, whether before or after the judgment or decree has been rendered;

(b) By certification from any court, at any time, of any question of law arising in a civil or criminal case on which the court seeks guidance. Upon such certification, the Supreme Court may issue binding instructions or may direct that the entire record of the case be transmitted for consideration.

Except as otherwise expressly provided, a prosecution may only be commenced for a felony offense—

(a) where punishable by death or pursuant to title 1, subtitle 1, chapter 1, Mayflower State Code, anytime after the commission of the offense;

(b) where involving the use, attempted use, or threatened use of physical force against another person or property of another, or any conspiracy to that effect, within fifty (50) days;

(c) where involving the intentional falsification or concealment of any material fact or the making of any materially false, fictitious, or fraudulent statement or representation to any employee of the government, within fifty (50) days;

(d) where involving misconduct on the part of any employee of the government—

(i) and posing a great risk of death or grievous bodily harm, within sixty (60) days;

(ii) in any such other case, within sixty (60) days;

(e) where involving the unauthorized distribution, possession, or sale of a firearm, within sixty (60) days; and

(f) in any such other case, within forty five (45) days.

Except as otherwise expressly provided, a prosecution may only be commenced for a misdemeanor offense—

(a) where involving the unauthorized distribution, possession, or sale of police-grade or non-civilian equipment, or misconduct on the part of any employee of the government, within forty (40) days;

(b) where involving the use, attempted use, or threatened use of physical force against another person or property of another, or any conspiracy to that effect, within forty-five (45) days; and

(c) in any such other case, within thirty (30) days.

For the purposes of this section, a prosecution is deemed commenced when—

(a) an indictment, information, or complaint is filed; or

(b) an arrest or bench warrant is issued, provided that the warrant names or describes the defendant with the same degree of particularity required for an indictment, information, or complaint.

For the purposes of this section, time starts to run on the day after the offense is committed, and does not run during a time where—

(a) the accused is not a resident or inhabitant of the State; or

(b) the accused hides himself within the State or otherwise renders himself non-amenable to processes being served upon him.

Nothing in this section shall be construed nor read so as to apply to offenses committed or prosecutions commenced before the date of enactment.

sections.text:
Homicide by any person is justifiable when:

(a) committed in defense of an immediate threat to another person's life;

(b) committed by lawful means during the course of a peace officer's duties;

(c) committed by a peace officer lawfully engaged in protective duties of a public official;

(d) committed by an individual lawfully authorized by state statute to use deadly force; or

(e) committed with the consent of the victim for the purpose of resetting their character.

(i) However, a person/victim cannot consent to being killed under this clause if: (1) they are in the lawful custody of a peace officer, correctional officer, security officer, or any other person lawfully detaining, arresting, or holding them in custody; or (2) they are incapable of knowingly or lawfully giving consent.

For the purposes of this act, the term “euthanasia” or “euthanization” shall refer to the successful act of committing a homicide or otherwise causing the death of another person in a manner wholly compliant with subchapter (II) of this act.

The term “authorized healthcare provider” shall refer to any employee, agent, or assign of the Lee County Regional Hospital, a private healthcare contractor for Lee County, Mayflower, who has completed training and maintains a Paramedic certification, or the RHC540 certification course; or any employee, agent, or assign of the same who holds a doctorate degree in medicine from any accredited university within the Union of Columbia.

Any person who conducts a lawful euthanization under the complete compliance of the provisions of this act shall be free from criminal liability and prosecution for the actions taken to conduct that euthanization, including freedom from liability for the crimes of:

(a) Any crime that would otherwise have been considered to be committed under Title 1, Subtitle 2 of the Mayflower State Code, excepting crimes committed that were reasonably unnecessary or otherwise medically irrelevant to the euthanization process.

(b) Any crime that would otherwise have been considered to be committed under Title 1, Subtitle 7, Chapter I of the Mayflower State Code, excepting crimes committed that were reasonably unnecessary or otherwise medically irrelevant to the euthanization process.

Euthanasia shall be considered lawful when conducted by any individual who is not an authorized healthcare provider when:

(a) The euthanization is undertaken with the explicit, stated consent of the euthanized;

(b) The euthanized is not actively being pursued or remanded into the custody of a law enforcement agency;

(c) The euthanized individual is suffering from a debilitating physical condition that prevents them from experiencing an adequate quality of life, such as total loss of physical movement or total loss of access to game mechanics;

(d) The euthanization is executed with a legally owned firearm.

(e) The euthanization is executed at a reasonable distance away from other individuals and the general public from the site of euthanization and the path of travel for any projectiles discharged from the firearm, or executed at the site of private property where the euthanizer and euthanized have a legal right to access; and

(f) The euthanization is conducted swiftly and as painlessly as reasonably possible by discharging the firearm into the euthanized’s head.

Euthanasia shall be considered lawful when conducted by an individual who is an authorized healthcare provider when:

(a) They are actively working in employment, on shift, are compensated for, or are otherwise representing their healthcare provider at the time of euthanization;

(b) The euthanization is undertaken with the explicit, stated consent of the euthanized, or their legal guardian or next of kin if they are indefinitely incapacitated or otherwise unable to consent.

(c) The euthanized individual is suffering from a debilitating physical condition that prevents them from experiencing an adequate quality of life, diagnosed by an authorized healthcare provider;

(d) The healthcare provider has not coaxed, suggested, encouraged, influenced, or otherwise unethically persuaded the euthanized or their guardian or next of kin to consent to euthanization when they reasonably would not have otherwise without such guidance;

(e) Alternative medical treatment methods have been extensively exhausted with no improvement in physical condition;

(f) The euthanization is executed with a legally owned firearm, medically controlled lethal injection, or the intentional overdosing of a medication to hasten or cause death;

(g) The euthanization is executed in a controlled and private environment within a place of hospice care, and only necessary medical personnel and the euthanized person’s direct related family are present or in observance;

(h) Reasonable efforts have been taken to prevent the risk of injury or death to any party other than the euthanized; and

(i) The euthanization is conducted as swiftly and as painlessly as reasonably possible by discharging the firearm into the euthanized’s head or the effective monitoring of the euthanized’s physical condition throughout the entire process and the administration of medication or substances that would not be reasonably known to cause pain.

Any individual who conducts a euthanization and subsequently faces law enforcement investigation, detainment, subpoena or other search and seizure shall bear the burden of proof for statutory compliance, and such proof shall evidently show the entire euthanization process from start to finish and the actions taken to meet all statutory requirements to render the euthanization lawful; and that no negligence occurred to where any statutory condition becomes unsatisfied.

Any individual who fails to meet any statutory requirement stipulated by this act when conducting a euthanization, or otherwise conducts a euthanization under a provision that they do not otherwise meet the conditions and authorization for shall not be protected under section (a) of this subchapter and shall face full criminal liability for any conduct that would otherwise be considered a criminal action notwithstanding euthanasia.

